! SEED=3
! GAME=../entharion/zcode-infocom/zork1-german-beta-r3-s880113.z5

# Zork I in German, beta release 3 / serial 880113 -- a rare
# translated Infocom build, and the only story in this corpus whose
# vocabulary needs characters the ASCII table does not have.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (Standard 3.8.5)
#
# ZSCII is not ASCII. Codes 155 to 251 are the "extra characters",
# and what each one means is the story's own business: a story may
# carry a Unicode translation table saying so, and one that does not
# falls back to the default table the Standard prints. A German
# story leans on that table for every umlaut and every sharp s.
#
# So "öffne" -- open -- is not a decorative flourish here. To match
# it against the dictionary the interpreter must turn that o-umlaut
# into its extra-character code, look the word up in that encoding,
# and hand the parser something the story recognizes. Get the table
# wrong, or fall back to ASCII, and the story answers that it does
# not know the word. This walk opens a mailbox, reads what is in it,
# walks around the house, opens the window, and climbs in -- all in
# German, with the umlaut carrying two of the commands.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE
#
# It does not guard the painted terminal on Windows, where non-ASCII
# input is still unreachable (issue #342): the console hands a byte
# read only the first byte of a multibyte character, so the umlaut
# never arrives to be encoded. A recording feeds its commands
# through the acceptance driver, not the keyboard, so this script
# would replay perfectly green on a face that cannot type a single
# one of these words. The encoding is what is under test here. The
# keyboard is a separate problem and needs a separate answer.

öffne briefkasten
lies zettel

norden
osten
öffne fenster
westen

nimm sack
öffne sack
inventar
